#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079617 :tutorial/content "Let's solve the following differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}^2 y}{\\mathrm{d} x^2} + 5\\frac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} x} + 6y =0\n$$\n\nFrom the previous slide, we know this ODE has a characteristic equation of the form $a\\lambda^2 + b\\lambda + c=0.$ Here, $a=1, b=5,$ and $c=6,$ so the characteristic equation is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+5\\lambda +6 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)(\\lambda +2) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe roots are $\\lambda_1 = -2$ and $\\lambda_2 = -3.$ Each root gives a particular solution to the ODE: $y_1=e^{-2x}$ and $y_2=e^{-3x}.$\n\nThe **general solution** is a linear combination of these particular solutions:\n\n$$\ny(x) = Ae^{-2x} + Be^{-3x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants." :tutorial/id #uuid "fe48b08c-e63b-577c-ba34-bc61bc5b2966" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14884 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079619 :tutorial/content "From the previous slide, the temperature of the thermometer is given by\n\n$$\nT = 20 + Ke^{-kt},\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ and $k$ are constants to be determined.\n\nWe're told that the initial temperature of the water was $100^\\circ\\textrm{C}.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0) = 100{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}100 & =20+K{e}^{0} \\\\ 100 & =20+K \\\\ K & =80\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following expression for $T(t){:}$\n\n$$\nT(t) = 20 + 80e^{-kt}\n$$\n\nWe can compute $k$ using the fact that $T(7)=60{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}60 & =20+80{e}^{-7k} \\\\ 40 & =80{e}^{-7k} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & ={e}^{-7k} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) & =-7k \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) & =-7k \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{7}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the temperature, in $^\\circ\\textrm{C},$ of the thermometer after $t$ minutes is\n\n$$\nT = 20 + 80e^{-t\\ln(2)/7}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "7811364e-9b00-5478-8f01-6f3ddfee6f58" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14906 :tutorial/title "Newton's Law of Cooling (Continued)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079670 :tutorial/content "A second-order, linear, homogeneous ordinary differential equation with constant coefficients has the general form\n\n$$\na\\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} x^2} + b\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} + cy =0,\n$$\n\nwhere $a, b,$ and $c$ are constants.\n\nTo find solutions, we can propose a trial solution of the form $y=e^{\\lambda x},$ where $\\lambda$ is a constant to be determined. Differentiating this gives:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\lambda e^{\\lambda x}, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}^2y}{\\textrm{d}x^2} = \\lambda^2 e^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the general ODE, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a({\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x})+b(\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x})+c({e}^{\\lambda x}) & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}(a{\\lambda }^{2}+b\\lambda +c) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $e^{\\lambda x}$ can never be zero, the expression in the parentheses must be zero:\n\n$$\na\\lambda^2+b\\lambda + c =0 .\n$$\n\nThis is called the **characteristic equation** (or **auxiliary equation**). The roots of this quadratic equation for $\\lambda$ give us the solutions to the original differential equation.\n\nNext, we'll use the characteristic equation to solve a specific example." :tutorial/id #uuid "c8c3fdb1-a293-55b7-bb3f-a3b91cd13c06" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4046 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079671 :tutorial/content "Now that we’ve practiced identifying inhibited decay models, let’s look at a classic example: **Newton’s law of cooling**.\n\nThis law describes how the temperature of an object changes when it is placed in an environment with a different temperature. Formally, it states that\n\n*The rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings.*\n\nThis leads to the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}T}{\\textrm{d}t} = -k(T-T_S),\n$$\n\nwhere $T_S$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nLet’s put this into action. Suppose a thermometer is placed in a pot of boiling water and reads $100^\\circ\\textrm{C}.$ The pot is then placed in a $20^\\circ\\textrm{C}$ room, and the thermometer's reading begins to decrease.\n\nGiven that after $7$ minutes its reading is $60^\\circ\\textrm{C},$ let's model the thermometer's temperature after $t$ minutes.\n\nIn this case, the temperature of the surroundings is $T_S = 20^\\circ\\textrm{C}.$ So, by Newton's law of cooling, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}T}{\\textrm{d}t} = -k(T-20),\n$$\n\nwhere $T(t)$ is the temperature reading of the thermometer and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}T}{T-20} & =-k\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}T}{T-20} & =-k\\int \\text{d}t \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-20| & =-kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-20 & ={e}^{-kt+C} \\\\ T-20 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T-20 & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T & =20+K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K=e^C$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe'll determine the value of $K$ and $k$ in the next slide." :tutorial/id #uuid "f58653bc-cafb-532a-b92d-8183054c181b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4075 :tutorial/title "Newton's Law of Cooling"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079684 :tutorial/content "In many real situations, a quantity decreases quickly at first, but then the decrease slows down and the quantity levels off near some fixed value.\n\nFor example, consider a cup of hot coffee placed in a room. At first, the coffee cools rapidly because it is much hotter than the surrounding air. As time passes and the coffee temperature gets closer to room temperature, the cooling slows down. Eventually, the temperature levels off and approaches the room temperature rather than continuing to decrease.\n\nThis type of behavior is described by an **inhibited decay model**.\n\nAn *inhibited decay equation* models situations where the rate at which a quantity $P$ decreases depends on how far it is from a target value $a$, called the **equilibrium value**. This equilibrium value represents the level that the quantity approaches over time.\n\nAn inhibited decay equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}P}{\\mathrm{d}t} = -r(P-a),\n$$\n\nand an inhibited decay initial value problem has the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}P}{\\mathrm{d}t} = -r(P-a), \\qquad P(0) = P_0\n$$\n\nwhere $r > 0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $P > a$ for all $t > 0.$\n\nIn this model, the equilibrium value $a$ acts as a lower bound. The solution decreases over time but never drops below $a,$ instead approaching it more and more slowly. The figure below illustrates this behavior.\n\n![](images/1b/1b68f547db4090382b3fee626d029c619448b1c9adafe8b50312d162ff85e623.png)\n\n**Note:** Inhibited growth and inhibited decay use the same basic idea: the rate of change is proportional to the difference between $P$ and the equilibrium value $a$. The only difference is whether the solution approaches $a$ from below (growth) or from above (decay).\n\nInhibited decay models can be written in expanded form. To identify an expanded equation as inhibited decay, we factorise out the *negative* coefficient of $P.$ For example, the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}P}{\\textrm{d}t} = 20-5P,\n$$\n\n*is* an inhibited decay equation for $P > 4$ and $t > 0,$ since it can be expressed with $r=5$ and $a=4$ as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}P}{\\textrm{d}t} = -5(P-4).\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "da7cd802-d46b-546f-bdc3-8a783a8bb999" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5513 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087839 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087515} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f88c1deb-cd11-5b68-b90b-2d8edfae2469" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Equation of a Wide Ellipse"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087840 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087516} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "de557d1d-5f27-50e3-bcba-22b21f83267f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Equation of a Tall Ellipse"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087841 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087517} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e43e393-8752-58bc-b87c-b72f679cbfd9" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Lengths of the Major and Minor Axes Given the Equation of an Ellipse"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087882 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087561} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ba25c0c-9ba9-586a-b66f-7f07076d2827" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Exponential Growth Problem"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087883 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087623} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e6330530-50c5-51e9-b616-2b97ce42cade" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Exponential Decay Problem"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087884 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087622} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7a63a0ae-3e16-5cdb-92f5-fea09351db84" :knowledge-point/title "Computing an Unknown Rate"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087886 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087456} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8edbecf-59d3-5f32-a0ac-d6e8f2128dc0" :knowledge-point/title "Transforming a Homogeneous Differential Equation into a Separable Equation Using a Substitution"}]}
